:[],"policy_area":"Immigration","related":{"companion":[],"related":[]},"selection":{"reason":"Impact scored and summarised by Gemini. (rated before the current eligibility rules; kept)","status":"analyzed"},"sources":{"latest":{"date":"2026-07-23","label":"Introduced in House","url":"https://www.congress.gov/119/bills/hr9944/BILLS-119hr9944ih.htm"},"other":[{"label":"This bill on Congress.gov","url":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/9944"}],"past":[]},"sponsor":{"chamber":"House","district":"25","id":"W000797","name":"Rep. Wasserman Schultz, Debbie","party":"D","state":"FL"},"stage":"in_committee","stage_label":"Referred to committee","summary":"H.R. 9944, titled the Temporary Protected Status Review Act or TPS Review Act, allows and sets rules for judicial review when the Secretary of Homeland Security terminates Temporary Protected Status (TPS) designations. It defines who can sue, how courts handle challenges, record-keeping requirements for the government, the burden of proof required, and how individuals retain their status while a lawsuit is ongoing.","summary_detail":{"affected_groups":[{"effect":"Can bring legal challenges against TPS terminations, keep their status and work authorization during lawsuits, and avoid removal or detention solely because of a challenged termination.","group":"Temporary Protected Status (TPS) applicants and beneficiaries"},{"effect":"Authorized to bring civil actions for judicial review of TPS terminations.","group":"Organizations representing TPS applicants or beneficiaries"},{"effect":"Authorized to bring civil actions alleging injury resulting from a challenged TPS termination.","group":"States and units of local government"},{"effect":"Must meet strict documentation, explanation, and clear-and-convincing evidence standards when terminating TPS designations, and is subject to judicial oversight and potential court orders to vacate terminations.","group":"Secretary of Homeland Security"}],"key_provisions":["Establishes that any termination of a TPS designation constitutes a final agency action subject to judicial review under chapter 7 of title 5, United States Code.","Allows TPS beneficiaries, classes of individuals, representative organizations, and states or local governments to bring a civil action for judicial review.","Requires the Secretary of Homeland Security to compile, certify, and preserve a complete administrative record including any dissenting analysis or inconsistent material evidence prior to issuing a termination.","Places the burden of proof on the Secretary of Homeland Security to establish by clear and convincing evidence that termination requirements are met.","Stays the termination of a TPS designation automatically upon the filing of a lawsuit, keeping affected individuals authorized to remain and automatically extending work permits.","Treats any determination by the Secretary to terminate, partially terminate, or materially reduce a TPS designation as a major rule under the Congressional Review Act."],"missing_information":["The source text does not specify exact cost estimates or appropriations related to administering the new judicial review processes."],"plain_summary":"H.R. 9944, titled the Temporary Protected Status Review Act or TPS Review Act, allows and sets rules for judicial review when the Secretary of Homeland Security terminates Temporary Protected Status (TPS) designations. It defines who can sue, how courts handle challenges, record-keeping requirements for the government, the burden of proof required, and how individuals retain their status while a lawsuit is ongoing.","significance":"This bill significantly alters the legal framework governing Temporary Protected Status by explicitly introducing sweeping judicial review rights, mandatory administrative recordkeeping, high evidentiary burdens for the government, and automatic stays of termination during legal challenges."},"summary_meta":{"generated_at":"2026-11-04T22:41:04